(window.LEARN_C = window.LEARN_C || {})["mth-11-03"] = {"6.2":{"s":"A circle is the set of points at a fixed distance, the radius, from a fixed point, the centre. With the centre at the origin the equation is x² + y² = r². With the centre at (h, k) it is (x − h)² + (y − k)² = r². Expanding gives the general form with 2gx and 2fy, whose centre is (−g, −f). The circle is real only if g² + f² is more than c.","f":[{"x":"x^2 + y^2 = r^2","n":"Standard circle with centre (0, 0) and radius r. In parametric form x = r*cos(theta), y = r*sin(theta).","r":"Balbharati Maths XI Part 1, p.136"},{"x":"(x - h)^2 + (y - k)^2 = r^2","n":"Centre-radius form: centre (h, k), radius r.","r":"Balbharati Maths XI Part 1, p.136"},{"x":"x^2 + y^2 + 2*g*x + 2*f*y + c = 0","n":"General form: centre (-g, -f), radius sqrt(g^2 + f^2 - c), real if g^2 + f^2 > c.","r":"Balbharati Maths XI Part 1, p.136"}],"w":["In the general form the centre is (−g, −f): the signs flip, and the coefficients are 2g and 2f, not g and f.","Radius = √(g² + f² − c). If this is negative, there is no real circle."],"x":["Think of a circle as the path of a point that stays at one fixed distance, the radius r, from a fixed point, the centre. If the centre is at the origin, the equation is x² + y² = r². For a centre at (h, k), it is (x − h)² + (y − k)² = r². To get this, say that the distance of the point (x, y) from (h, k) is r, and square both sides.","The general equation of a circle is x² + y² + 2gx + 2fy + c = 0. By completing the squares, it becomes (x + g)² + (y + f)² = g² + f² − c. So the centre is (−g, −f), and the radius is √(g² + f² − c). The expression g² + f² − c must be positive for a real circle. If it is zero, the circle shrinks to a point, and if it is negative, there is no real circle.","To find the centre and the radius of a circle given as x² + y² − 6x + 8y − 11 = 0, read off 2g = −6 and 2f = 8, so g = −3 and f = 4. The centre is (3, −4), and the radius is √(9 + 16 + 11) = 6. In the same way, the equation of the circle with the ends of a diameter at (x₁, y₁) and (x₂, y₂) is (x − x₁)(x − x₂) + (y − y₁)(y − y₂) = 0.","To find a circle through three points, put each point in the general equation, and solve the three equations for g, f and c. Common mistake: taking the centre as (g, f), instead of (−g, −f). Check that the coefficients of x² and y² are both 1 before you read off g and f."],"e":"Circle with a diameter from (2, 3) to (6, 7): the centre is (4, 5) and the radius is half of √32, which is 2.83. Its equation is (x − 2)(x − 6) + (y − 3)(y − 7) = 0, that is x² + y² − 8x − 10y + 33 = 0."},"6.3":{"s":"A tangent to a circle touches it at exactly one point, and it is perpendicular to the radius at that point. For the circle x² + y² = r², the tangent at a point (x1, y1) on it is found by replacing x² by x·x1 and y² by y·y1. In parametric form, the tangent at the point with parameter θ1 is also a short linear equation. The slope form of a tangent has the slope m given.","f":[{"x":"x*x_1 + y*y_1 = r^2","n":"Tangent to x^2 + y^2 = r^2 at the point (x_1, y_1) on the circle.","r":"Balbharati Maths XI Part 1, p.136"},{"x":"x*cos(theta_1) + y*sin(theta_1) = r","n":"Tangent at the point with parameter theta_1, using x = r*cos(theta), y = r*sin(theta).","r":"Balbharati Maths XI Part 1, p.136"},{"x":"y = m*x ± sqrt(r^2*m^2 + r^2)","n":"Tangent of slope m to x^2 + y^2 = r^2: two parallel tangents.","r":"Balbharati Maths XI Part 1, p.136"}],"w":["The point must lie on the circle; for a point outside, two tangents can be drawn.","The slope form has two signs: there are two tangents with the same slope, one on each side."],"x":["A tangent to a circle is a line that touches the circle at exactly one point, called the point of contact. The tangent is perpendicular to the radius at the point of contact. This fact is the key to all the problems on tangents.","For the circle x² + y² = r², the tangent at the point (x₁, y₁) on the circle is xx₁ + yy₁ = r². If the point is given by the angle θ, as (r cos θ, r sin θ), the tangent is x cos θ + y sin θ = r. For the circle x² + y² + 2gx + 2fy + c = 0, the tangent at (x₁, y₁) is xx₁ + yy₁ + g(x + x₁) + f(y + y₁) + c = 0. The rule is to replace x² by xx₁, y² by yy₁, 2x by x + x₁, and 2y by y + y₁.","The length of the tangent from an outside point (x₁, y₁) to the circle x² + y² = r² is √(x₁² + y₁² − r²). It comes from the right triangle formed by the centre, the point of contact and the outside point. The tangent to x² + y² = 25 at (3, 4) is 3x + 4y = 25, and it cuts the x axis at (25/3, 0).","Common mistakes: using the formula for a point that is not on the circle (first check that the point satisfies the equation), and forgetting that there are two tangents from an outside point."],"e":"The tangent from (5, 0) to the circle x² + y² = 9 has the length √(25 − 9) = 4. The point of contact is (1.8, 2.4). Check: 1.8² + 2.4² = 3.24 + 5.76 = 9, so it is on the circle."},"6.4":{"s":"The line y = mx + c touches the circle x² + y² = r² only when c has a particular size. Put the line into the circle, or use the fact that the distance from the centre to a tangent equals the radius, and the condition follows. The same condition gives the slope form of the tangent, and the point where the line touches the circle can be found from it.","f":[{"x":"c^2 = r^2*m^2 + r^2","n":"Condition for y = m*x + c to be tangent to x^2 + y^2 = r^2. The point of contact is (-r^2*m/c, r^2/c).","r":"Balbharati Maths XI Part 1, p.136"}],"w":["The condition has r² on both terms: c² = r²(m² + 1). A common slip is to leave out the second r²."],"x":["A line is a tangent to a circle when its distance from the centre is equal to the radius. If the distance is less than the radius, the line cuts the circle at two points (a secant). If it is more, the line does not meet the circle.","For the circle x² + y² = r² and the line y = mx + c, the distance of the origin from the line mx − y + c = 0 is |c|/√(1 + m²). Setting this equal to r gives the condition for tangency: c² = r²(1 + m²) = r²m² + r². So the lines y = mx ± r√(1 + m²) are the two tangents to the circle with the slope m. They are parallel and on the opposite sides of the centre.","To find the tangent with the given slope, put the slope in c = ± r√(1 + m²). For the circle x² + y² = 16 and the slope 1, c = ±4√2 = ±5.657. To test whether a given line is a tangent, put the line into the equation of the circle. A single repeated root, with the discriminant zero, shows that the line touches the circle.","The length of the chord cut by a line at a distance p from the centre is 2√(r² − p²). Common mistake: forgetting the ± sign, so that only one of the two tangents is found."],"e":"For which values of c is y = 2x + c a tangent to x² + y² = 5? c² = 5(1 + 4) = 25, so c = ±5. A line at the distance 3 from the centre of the circle x² + y² = 25 cuts a chord of the length 2√(25 − 9) = 8."},"6.5":{"s":"The director circle of a conic is the path of the points from which two perpendicular tangents can be drawn to it. For a circle with centre at the origin and radius r, the director circle has the same centre and a larger radius, √2 times r. So its equation has 2r² on the right-hand side. The two tangents meet at right angles at every point of this circle.","f":[{"x":"x^2 + y^2 = 2*r^2","n":"Director circle of the circle x^2 + y^2 = r^2.","r":"Balbharati Maths XI Part 1, p.136"}],"w":["The director circle has radius r√2, so the right side is 2r², not 2r."],"x":["From an outside point P, two tangents can be drawn to a circle. The director circle of a circle is the locus of the points from which the two tangents to the circle are perpendicular to each other.","To find its equation for the circle x² + y² = r², take a point P from which the two tangents are at right angles. Then the quadrilateral formed by P, the two points of contact and the centre O has four right angles, and two adjacent sides equal to r, so it is a square. Its diagonal OP is r√2. So the point P is at the distance r√2 from the centre, and the director circle is x² + y² = 2r². It is a circle with the same centre, and a radius of r√2.","For a general circle with the centre (h, k) and the radius r, the director circle has the same centre, and the radius r√2: (x − h)² + (y − k)² = 2r². The director circle of x² + y² = 25 has the radius 5√2 = 7.07.","Common mistake: using a radius of 2r or r/√2 for the director circle. A check: the angle between the tangents from a point at the distance d is 2 sin⁻¹(r/d), and this is 90° when d = r√2."],"e":"From the point (5, 5), the tangents to x² + y² = 25 are perpendicular, since 5² + 5² = 50 = 2 × 25, so the point is on the director circle. The tangents are x = 5 and y = 5."}};
